WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.240
Have you ever stopped and wondered why our society

00:02.240 --> 00:05.200
kind of has a default setting for who's considered normal?

00:05.200 --> 00:07.480
It's a huge question, and it gets right to the heart

00:07.480 --> 00:09.920
of how our differences have been shaped by history

00:09.920 --> 00:11.080
and by power.

00:11.080 --> 00:12.960
So today, we're gonna dig into the tools

00:12.960 --> 00:15.540
that built this system, and maybe explore what it would take

00:15.540 --> 00:17.600
to build something totally new.

00:17.600 --> 00:20.160
And we're gonna start with this really powerful idea

00:20.160 --> 00:22.440
from the writer and activist Audrey Lord.

00:22.440 --> 00:24.800
What she's telling us here is that our identities,

00:24.800 --> 00:27.460
well, they aren't simple, they're not singular,

00:27.460 --> 00:31.060
they're complex, they're messy, they're all woven together.

00:31.060 --> 00:32.780
And that's a really crucial starting point

00:32.780 --> 00:34.900
for the journey we're about to take.

00:34.900 --> 00:37.420
Okay, so to really get how we got here,

00:37.420 --> 00:41.000
we have to start with a core concept from Lord herself.

00:41.000 --> 00:43.060
It's an idea that quietly shapes

00:43.060 --> 00:44.860
almost every part of our lives,

00:44.860 --> 00:47.180
whether we even realize it or not.

00:47.180 --> 00:48.940
She called it the mythical norm.

00:48.940 --> 00:52.020
And that word, mythical, is so important.

00:52.020 --> 00:55.540
This default person, you know, white, male, straight,

00:55.580 --> 00:59.580
Christian, well off, doesn't really exist as the majority,

00:59.580 --> 01:02.420
but it holds this incredible amount of power.

01:02.420 --> 01:04.320
It becomes the invisible measuring stick

01:04.320 --> 01:06.460
that everyone else is compared to.

01:06.460 --> 01:09.540
And if you fall outside that super narrow definition,

01:09.540 --> 01:13.340
you're automatically seen as different, as other.

01:13.340 --> 01:15.460
So what happens when we meet people

01:15.460 --> 01:17.020
who don't fit that norm?

01:17.020 --> 01:19.780
Well, Lordy argued that we've basically been programmed

01:19.780 --> 01:21.940
with a really limited set of options.

01:21.940 --> 01:23.540
Instead of having a rich toolbox

01:23.540 --> 01:25.020
for how to connect with each other,

01:25.020 --> 01:28.340
we're left with just a few, well, blunt instruments.

01:28.340 --> 01:31.500
Our default settings are pretty stark when you lay them out.

01:31.500 --> 01:33.860
We either pretend the difference doesn't exist,

01:33.860 --> 01:36.700
or we try to copy the dominant form to fit in.

01:36.700 --> 01:38.260
Or in the worst cases,

01:38.260 --> 01:40.980
we try to get rid of whatever we see as being beneath us.

01:40.980 --> 01:43.020
These are the deep, deep patterns

01:43.020 --> 01:45.860
that Lord is asking us to take a hard look at.

01:45.860 --> 01:50.020
And this, right here, this is the core of her message.

01:50.020 --> 01:52.660
The problem isn't that we're all different from one another.

01:52.660 --> 01:54.180
Of course we are.

01:54.180 --> 01:57.420
The real problem is our failure to see those differences,

01:57.420 --> 02:00.820
to understand them, and ultimately to value them.

02:00.820 --> 02:04.380
It's the refusal to engage that creates all the division.

02:04.380 --> 02:07.460
So how in the world does a mythical norm get built

02:07.460 --> 02:09.620
and then force on a global scale?

02:09.620 --> 02:11.100
I mean, an idea this big

02:11.100 --> 02:13.300
doesn't just pop out of thin air, right?

02:13.300 --> 02:14.940
A quick look into colonial history

02:14.940 --> 02:17.660
shows the very real and often brutal ways

02:17.660 --> 02:19.260
these ideas play out.

02:19.260 --> 02:20.820
We're going to look at this through the lens

02:20.820 --> 02:23.420
of something called political homophobia.

02:23.460 --> 02:25.820
Now, this isn't just about personal prejudice.

02:25.820 --> 02:29.340
This is about the deliberate strategic use of fear and hatred

02:29.340 --> 02:31.340
to achieve political goals.

02:31.340 --> 02:33.340
It's a way to build a national identity,

02:33.340 --> 02:35.100
shut down anyone who disagrees,

02:35.100 --> 02:37.780
and keep power in the hands of a small group,

02:37.780 --> 02:40.060
a chillingly effective tool.

02:40.060 --> 02:42.660
Okay, so in many post-colonial countries,

02:42.660 --> 02:44.180
like Nigeria, for example,

02:44.180 --> 02:46.300
you'll hear a common political argument

02:46.300 --> 02:48.660
that laws criminalizing homosexuality

02:48.660 --> 02:51.900
are just about defending traditional authentic values.

02:51.940 --> 02:53.860
But when historians dig into it,

02:53.860 --> 02:55.940
they find a completely different story.

02:55.940 --> 02:57.860
These laws aren't indigenous at all.

02:57.860 --> 03:00.460
They are a direct hangover from the British Empire.

03:01.300 --> 03:03.220
Let's just trace the journey here.

03:03.220 --> 03:05.740
It starts way back in 16th century England

03:05.740 --> 03:07.940
as a political weapon for a king.

03:07.940 --> 03:10.900
Then centuries later, a version of that law,

03:10.900 --> 03:12.580
a version that was actually rejected

03:12.580 --> 03:13.980
for use in Britain itself,

03:13.980 --> 03:16.180
gets written up for a colony in Australia.

03:16.180 --> 03:19.060
And from there, it's imposed on Nigeria.

03:19.060 --> 03:21.580
This was never a reflection of local values.

03:21.580 --> 03:24.500
It was a pure export of colonial power.

03:24.500 --> 03:25.900
And this brings us right back

03:25.900 --> 03:28.780
to one of Audre Lorde's most famous lines.

03:28.780 --> 03:30.140
The colonial legal system,

03:30.140 --> 03:32.540
with all its imposed rules and values,

03:32.540 --> 03:34.420
became the master's tools.

03:34.420 --> 03:36.260
They were designed for one purpose,

03:36.260 --> 03:38.780
to build and maintain the master's house,

03:38.780 --> 03:40.740
the structure of the empire.

03:40.740 --> 03:43.140
And what was the justification for all this?

03:43.140 --> 03:45.100
Well, this quote from a colonial officer

03:45.100 --> 03:46.580
lays it out pretty clearly.

03:46.580 --> 03:49.180
It's this deeply paternalistic worldview, right?

03:49.180 --> 03:51.660
Seeing the people they colonized, not as equals,

03:51.660 --> 03:54.300
but as children who needed parental control.

03:54.300 --> 03:55.740
This was the elitist mindset

03:55.740 --> 03:56.980
that was used to rationalize

03:56.980 --> 03:59.820
the whole project of colonial domination.

03:59.820 --> 04:02.500
So we've seen how these massive power structures

04:02.500 --> 04:03.940
were built throughout history.

04:03.940 --> 04:05.620
But what does it actually feel like

04:05.620 --> 04:07.340
to live inside them today?

04:07.340 --> 04:09.300
Especially when you exist at the crossroads

04:09.300 --> 04:11.220
of more than one identity.

04:11.220 --> 04:12.900
For that, we can turn to the work

04:12.900 --> 04:15.700
of the brilliant legal scholar, Kimberly Crenshaw.

04:15.700 --> 04:18.060
She gave us the term intersectionality.

04:18.060 --> 04:19.620
And it's basically a lens,

04:19.620 --> 04:22.540
a way of seeing the different forms of discrimination,

04:22.540 --> 04:24.380
like racism and sexism,

04:24.380 --> 04:26.180
don't just exist next to each other.

04:26.180 --> 04:28.300
They overlap, they interact,

04:28.300 --> 04:29.700
they pile on top of each other,

04:29.700 --> 04:33.020
creating totally unique experiences of bias.

04:33.020 --> 04:34.580
This quote from Roberto Valdez

04:34.580 --> 04:38.340
just makes that whole academic idea so personal and real.

04:38.340 --> 04:41.100
It's this feeling of pressure to chop up your own identity,

04:41.100 --> 04:42.620
to decide which part of yourself

04:42.620 --> 04:44.100
you're allowed to bring into a room,

04:44.100 --> 04:45.780
because you know the world isn't set up

04:45.780 --> 04:47.820
to see all of you at once.

04:47.820 --> 04:50.660
And this court case shows the real devastating harm

04:50.660 --> 04:52.460
of ignoring intersectionality.

04:52.460 --> 04:53.980
You have five black women

04:53.980 --> 04:56.380
who argued they were fired because of discrimination.

04:56.380 --> 04:58.020
But the court told them, sorry,

04:58.020 --> 05:00.540
you can't be discriminated against as black women.

05:00.540 --> 05:02.160
They said, you have to pick one.

05:02.160 --> 05:04.500
Was it racism or was it sexism?

05:04.500 --> 05:07.500
The legal system literally couldn't see their whole identity

05:07.500 --> 05:09.700
and because of that, they lost their case.

05:09.700 --> 05:11.220
This is the exact problem

05:11.220 --> 05:13.580
Crenshaw and Lorde were talking about.

05:13.580 --> 05:16.620
Okay, so if the old ways of ignoring, copying

05:16.620 --> 05:19.540
or destroying difference are failing us so badly,

05:19.540 --> 05:20.660
what's the alternative?

05:20.660 --> 05:22.140
Where do we go from here?

05:22.140 --> 05:24.540
Let's circle back one last time to Audrey Lorde

05:24.540 --> 05:26.660
for her vision of the future.

05:26.660 --> 05:30.180
Lorde's answer was so clear, it was almost revolutionary.

05:30.180 --> 05:32.860
She said our very survival depends on our ability

05:32.860 --> 05:35.140
to connect across our differences

05:35.140 --> 05:37.700
and to see each other as equals while doing it.

05:37.700 --> 05:40.740
Not in spite of our differences, but with them.

05:40.740 --> 05:43.260
And she even laid out a kind of path forward.

05:43.260 --> 05:45.500
It starts with us, with doing the hard work

05:45.540 --> 05:48.700
of unlearning the toxic stuff society has taught us.

05:48.700 --> 05:51.740
Then it moves outward to really see the differences

05:51.740 --> 05:54.380
among our equals and then to use those differences

05:54.380 --> 05:55.940
not as a wall between us,

05:55.940 --> 05:58.300
but as a source of creativity and strength

05:58.300 --> 06:02.820
and ultimately to completely redefine what power even is.

06:02.820 --> 06:05.140
And all of this leads to her most famous,

06:05.140 --> 06:06.860
most enduring warning.

06:06.860 --> 06:09.220
You cannot fix a system of oppression

06:09.220 --> 06:10.940
using the exact same logic

06:10.940 --> 06:13.100
that created it in the first place.

06:13.100 --> 06:15.140
Trying to fit into the mythical norm

06:15.180 --> 06:17.780
or using tools that refuse to see your whole self

06:17.780 --> 06:20.020
is never going to set you free.

06:20.020 --> 06:22.180
You can't dismantle the master's house

06:22.180 --> 06:24.260
with the master's tools.

06:24.260 --> 06:26.700
So in the end, Lorde leaves us with a challenge,

06:26.700 --> 06:28.140
not a simple answer.

06:28.140 --> 06:30.860
If the old tools of division and hierarchy

06:30.860 --> 06:33.180
and erasure are never gonna work,

06:33.180 --> 06:36.980
then the real question, the one for all of us is this,

06:36.980 --> 06:39.460
what new tools are we gonna build together?

